rge of&nbsp;&nbsp; looking for moles. But they're exempting the&nbsp; executives. Oh, you know, because after all,&nbsp;&nbsp; it's not like a high-ranking executive of the FBI&nbsp; has ever been a mole. Oh, never. Right. Never,&nbsp;&nbsp; never would that happen.May I interrupt you for&nbsp; a second? I went to an event last night. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp; book launch for the two IRS whistleblowers who&nbsp; blew the whistle on Hunter Biden's income-tax&nbsp;&nbsp; evasion, right? They were specifically told,&nbsp; "Don't look at the laptop." And they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp; "What, are they crazy? Their bosses?"Of course&nbsp; we're going to look at the laptop, and they found&nbsp;&nbsp; that he spent $10,000 at a sex club and tried to&nbsp; write it off as a deduction, line-iteming it as&nbsp;&nbsp; golf-club membership, right? A golf country-club&nbsp; membership. It was a sex club. He spent $10,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp; one night in a sex club. I was going to say, like,&nbsp; how much sex can one man have? Well, when you're&nbsp;&nbsp; on that much meth...I could see having $6,000. But&nbsp; the reason I'm bringing this up is, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp; these guys were very kind to me last night, and&nbsp; Brad Birkenfeld was there. There were a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp; of whistleblowers that we inspired to go public.&nbsp; They knew it was going to cost them their careers.&nbsp;&nbsp; But Senator Grassley was there. Chairman Comer&nbsp; was there. Chairman Jordan was there, and the&nbsp;&nbsp; Secretary of the Treasury, Yellen, was there. And&nbsp; I'll tell you what, man—when I blew the whistle on&nbsp;&nbsp; the torture program, I stood alone. And now the&nbsp; fact that there are all these political people&nbsp;&nbsp; saying it's unacceptable to treat whistleblowers&nbsp; this way—I loved it. Now the bad part is we still&nbsp;&nbsp; need a shield law. And the bad part of this is&nbsp; every one of those people I just mentioned is&nbsp;&nbsp; a Republican.It looks like I froze. 