inute and&nbsp; then decided it wasn't for them—like Porter Goss.&nbsp;&nbsp; Porter Goss was in the CIA for four years. He was&nbsp; a failed case officer.He moved to Florida, became&nbsp;&nbsp; a multimillionaire real-estate developer, and then&nbsp; got himself elected to Congress, became chairman&nbsp;&nbsp; of the Intelligence Committee, and then went&nbsp; back to be an absolutely terrible—historically&nbsp;&nbsp; terrible—CIA director. But there was a time when&nbsp; that was really highly unusual. And now it seems&nbsp;&nbsp; like people at the CIA—everybody envisions himself&nbsp; or herself as governor of Virginiaor a member of&nbsp;&nbsp; Congress. I remember when Will Hurd resigned&nbsp; to run for Congress. I said, "Well, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp; that's not really a step up to run for Congress.&nbsp; What are you doing?" And he ran. He won. He&nbsp;&nbsp; won three times. They tried to recruit me to run&nbsp; for Congress, and I told him the same thing. Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp; Will won three times. He stepped down. Yeah.&nbsp; And then quit.Thanks for the dollar and the&nbsp;&nbsp; comment. Also, thanks to Hyperneurotic for the two&nbsp; dollars and the advice about avoiding packet loss,&nbsp;&nbsp; which I still don't really understand. Here's a&nbsp; question for you, John. Okay, this is from Fla—as&nbsp;&nbsp; I'm going to pronounce it—6624. When an agency&nbsp; recruits someone as high-level as a president,&nbsp;&nbsp; how do you keep them in check? I would think that&nbsp; they don't need the money anymore. An example&nbsp;&nbsp; would be Qaddafi.That's a great question.&nbsp; And it's a rather important question.&nbsp;&nbsp; Nine times out of ten, you actually recruit&nbsp; the person when they're a junior-level nobody,&nbsp;&nbsp; and then they rise through the system and&nbsp; they become president or prime minister or&nbsp;&nbsp; defense minister or foreign minister. When that&nbsp; happens, you just kind of put them on ice. You&nbsp;&nbsp; cut off contact while they're president or&nbsp; prime minister. And if they have something&nbsp;&nbsp; important to say,they'll ask to see the U.S.&nbsp; ambassador. The U.S. ambassador knows that's&nbsp;&nbsp; a signal to bring you along. And then you collect&nbsp; the intelligence while the ambassador pretends not&nbsp;&nbsp; to listen in the corner of the room. Otherwise,&nbsp; if it's somebody like Qaddafi, for example—or&nbsp;&nbsp; now we know Hamid Karzai—the CIA station&nbsp; chief just goes straight into the office&nbsp;&nbsp; and does the meeting.You know, suitcases full&nbsp; of money and all that good stuff. But how do&nbsp;&nbsp; you keep that person—you know, how do you run&nbsp; them without them getting out of control?Well,&nbsp;&nbsp; look at Manuel Noriega. He went out of&nbsp; control. And you see what the U.S. response&nbsp;&nbsp; was. Right. The U.S. response was to essentially&nbsp; overthrow him, kidnap him, occupy his country,&nbsp;&nbsp; and put him in prison for the rest of his life.&nbsp; And that's really what it comes down to. It's&nbsp;&nbsp; like, "You're fired."Thanks, Toby, for the two&nbsp; Australian dollars and the thought. Agree. Pardon,&nbsp;&nbsp; Marble—thanks for the $4.99. Swirling green&nbsp; indeed."Swimming green" is people—just remember&nbsp;&nbsp; that. It's very important to remember that. Thanks&nbsp; for the five bucks to 7371Mojor. Just heard about&nbsp;&nbsp; the Huckabee-Pollard story from you guys first—and&nbsp; also Pod Save America. It's not in any of my news&nbsp;&nbsp; apps. Why do you think that is?See, that's a good&nbsp; question. That is a good question. It's always&nbsp;&nbsp; like I say—it's never about what they say. Well,&nbsp; it's usually not about what they say. It's usually&nbsp;&nbsp; about what they don't say.Yeah. Yeah. It should be&nbsp; above-the-fold, page one of every news publication&nbsp;&nbsp; in America today. And you're right—it's not.&nbsp; Morrow, thanks for the five bucks. Any thoughts&nbsp;&