ave their economy die on. The whole thing is&nbsp; weird. Nord Stream is weird—how it went down,&nbsp;&nbsp; the whole thing. But anyway—so currently,&nbsp; Zelensky—we've talked about this; everybody&nbsp;&nbsp; who's with us on this show knows that he has this&nbsp; buddy, Timur Mindik, who's basically running the&nbsp;&nbsp; Enron of Ukraine and causing rolling blackouts. He&nbsp; and his minions are basically suspected ofstealing&nbsp;&nbsp; at least a hundred million—maybe more—from&nbsp; Ukraine. And so far Zelensky himself—dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp; right—from the investigation. But you know,&nbsp; Zelensky is obviously politically weak. He's back&nbsp;&nbsp; on his heels right now, and so a lot of people&nbsp; are speculating—and I kind of agree with their&nbsp;&nbsp; speculation—that Trump used this as an opportunity&nbsp; to kind ofpush through a deal that otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp; Ukraine might not agree to. And basically it's the&nbsp; deal that we kind of knew all along. It's mostly a&nbsp;&nbsp; deal that favors Russia, where the existing lines&nbsp; now—John, what's happening is the existing lines&nbsp;&nbsp; are kind of the Ukrainians accept that they've&nbsp; lost that territory and it's never coming back.&nbsp;&nbsp; Now they're saying that they don't want to cede&nbsp; additional territory that is not yet under Russian&nbsp;&nbsp; control. Right.Isn't this exactly what we have all&nbsp; said from the very beginning—that the Ukrainians&nbsp;&nbsp; are going to have to walk away from the territory&nbsp; where most people are native Russian speakers? So&nbsp;&nbsp; Donetsk and Luhansk—they're going to have to walk&nbsp; away from Crimea, which has only been Ukrainian&nbsp;&nbsp; since like 1953 anyway. Right.We've all said&nbsp; that from the very beginning, and they couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp; join NATO, but they could fast-track the European&nbsp; Union. And that's exactly what this thing's going&nbsp;&nbsp; to turn out to be—not maybe demilitarization, but&nbsp; reduced militarization. By the way, that's Russia&nbsp;&nbsp; and the U.S. doing Ukraine a favor. I wish someone&nbsp; would tell the U.S. to cut its military in half so&nbsp;&nbsp; that we could start feeding the homeless.&nbsp; Seriously. Totally agree.So I mean, like,&nbsp;&nbsp; you know—you don't want to be like North Korea&nbsp; where all of your resources go into the military.&nbsp;&nbsp; But anyway—so all right. So how this looks&nbsp; like—obviously the Trump administration is putting&nbsp;&nbsp; some oomph behind it, right? They're sending the&nbsp; Secretary of the Army and General Randy George off&nbsp;&nbsp; to Kyiv. That's a big deal. Right. So it seems&nbsp; to me like—and we had Trump today or yesterday&nbsp;&nbsp; saying, you know, "I've alreadyfixed eight wars.&nbsp; Now I'm working on the one." So funny. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp; maybe tell the Palestinians who keep getting blown&nbsp; up every day. He confuses ceasefires for the ends&nbsp;&nbsp; of wars. Yeah, they're not the same thing. Not the&nbsp; same thing. But, you know, I mean, look—ceasefires&nbsp;&nbsp; are always appreciated. But nevertheless.&nbsp; So all right. So what happens now? I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp; it looks likeZelensky is chastened. It looks like&nbsp; he's more—I mean, but on the other hand, you never&nbsp;&nbsp; know with him because he's such a weasel. You&nbsp; know, over the last year or so we've seen him&nbsp;&nbsp; go from like, "Oh no, I'm serious about peace" to&nbsp; "But I'm only serious about peace if I don't have&nbsp;&nbsp; to give up any territory at all." You know, the&nbsp; thing about Zelensky—I think he's at the point now&nbsp;&nbsp; wherehe's wondering, number one, if he can make&nbsp; it to the end of the Trump administration—which&nbsp;&nbsp; he can't; we have three years to go—and number&nbsp; two, even if he could, will another Republican&nbsp;&nbsp; win and put him out of business, or will a&nbsp; Democrat come in and rescue him and save him?&nbsp;&nbsp; I learn